      name: 'Lewin’s change model (unfreeze, change, refreeze)', short: 'Lewin’s change model', who: 'Kurt Lewin, “Frontiers in Group Dynamics” (1947), where he called it a three-step procedure', kind: 'ordered', unit: 'stages',
      items: [
        { t: 'Unfreeze', d: 'Create readiness: make the need to move felt and loosen the current equilibrium.' },
        { t: 'Change', d: 'Move to the new level: people adopt the new behaviors, processes, or structures.' },
        { t: 'Refreeze', d: 'Stabilize the new state so it becomes the norm.' },
      ],
      orderNote: 'first to last',
      decoys: ['Diagnose', 'Pilot', 'Evaluate'],
      hook: 'An ice cube: melt it, reshape it, freeze it again.',
      exam: 'Questions say “change model” or “unfreeze” to set it apart from Lewin’s leadership styles (autocratic, democratic, laissez-faire). ATD’s blog suggests Lewin’s model for organizations that need a less sophisticated approach to change.',
      note: 'Lewin’s own 1947 terms were unfreezing, moving, and freezing; “refreezing” is now the usual name for the last step. Sources call the middle stage change, moving, or transition. Lewin’s force field analysis belongs with this model.',
